      "mastery": 0.3999009145171569,
      "prior_failures": 0,
      "text": "The gradient of the tangent at a point is what the gradients of the secants approach as the second point slides in towards the first. A secant taken at any finite separation is still a secant: its gradient is close to the tangent's, not equal to it. For y = x^2 the limit works out to 2x.",
      "opening": false,
      "version": 773
    },
    {
      "item_id": "ca_tan_p1",
      "concept_id": "limit_concept",
      "move": "remediate",
      "level": "targeted",
      "targets": "tangent_as_secant",
      "mastery": 0.3999009145171569,
      "prior_failures": 0,
      "text": "The expression should represent the difference in y-values between (5 + h) and 5, which is ((5 + h)^2 - 5^2). The current denominator also needs to account for the change in x.",
      "opening": false,
      "version": 778
    },
    {
      "item_id": "ca_tan_p1",
      "concept_id": "limit_concept",
      "move": "remediate",
      "level": "worked_step",
      "targets": "tangent_as_secant",
      "mastery": 0.3999009145171569,
      "prior_failures": 1,
      "text": "To find the gradient of the secant, use the formula (y2 - y1) / (x2 - x1).\n\nIdentify the coordinates for the first point: (5, 5^2).\nIdentify the coordinates for the second point: (5 + h, (5 + h)^2).\nExpand the term (5 + h)^2.\nSubtract the y-values in the numerator.\nDetermine the difference between the x-values in the denominator.",
      "opening": false,
      "version": 781
    },
    {
      "item_id": "ca_tan_p1",
      "concept_id": "limit_concept",
      "move": "remediate",
      "level": "worked_step",
      "targets": "tangent_as_secant",
      "mastery": 0.3999009145171569,
      "prior_failures": 2,
      "text": "To find the gradient of the secant, use the formula (y2 - y1) / (x2 - x1).\n\ny2 = (5 + h)^2\ny1 = 25\nx2 = 5 + h\nx1 = 5\n\nExpand the expression for y2.\nSubtract y1 from y2 in the numerator.\nSubtract x1 from x2 in the denominator.",
      "opening": false,
      "version": 784
    },
    {
      "item_id": "",
      "concept_id": "limit_concept",
      "move": "explain",
      "level": "socratic",
      "targets": null,
      "mastery": 0.0,
      "prior_failures": 4,
      "text": "To find the gradient of a line, how many points on that line do you usually need to know?",
      "opening": true,
      "version": 785
    },
    {
      "item_id": "",
      "concept_id": "limit_concept",
      "move": "explain",
      "level": "socratic",
      "targets": null,
      "mastery": 0.0,
      "prior_failures": 4,
      "text": "That's correct; normally we use two points to calculate a slope. However, if we want to find the gradient of a curve at just one specific point, how many points are we technically 'given' in that scenario?",
      "opening": false,
      "version": 787
    },
    {
      "item_id": "",
      "concept_id": "limit_concept",
      "move": "explain",
      "level": "socratic",
      "targets": null,
      "mastery": 0.0,
      "prior_failures": 4,
      "text": "You hit on exactly why this is such a tricky concept! Since we only have one point, we don't have the two points usually required to calculate a gradient. \n\nIf we pick a second point very close to our first one and connect them, what kind of line do we create?",
      "opening": false,
      "version": 789
    },
    {
      "item_id": "",
      "concept_id": "limit_concept",
      "move": "explain",
      "level": "socratic",
      "targets": null,
      "mastery": 0.0,
      "prior_failures": 4,
      "text": "That line connecting two separate points on a curve is called a secant line.\n\nEven though it's just an approximation of the curve's direction at that specific spot, what do you think happens to the gradient of that secant as we move the second point closer and closer to the first one?",
      "opening": false,
      "version": 791
    },
    {
      "item_id": "",
      "concept_id": "limit_concept",
      "move": "explain",
      "level": "summary",
      "targets": null,
      "mastery": 0.0,
      "prior_failures": 4,
      "text": "The gradient of a curve at a single point. A gradient normally needs two points, so at first this looks like it is asking for something impossible.\n\nTake a second point nearby and you get a secant, whose gradient you can work out the ordinary way. Slide that second point in towards the first and the secant gradients settle towards a number. No finite separation gives it exactly \u2014 a secant is always a secant \u2014 but the value they approach is perfectly well defined, and that limit is what 'the gradient at a point' means.\n\nThe gradient of the tangent at a point is what the gradients of the secants approach as the second point slides in towards the first. A secant taken at any finite separation is still a secant: its gradient is close to the tangent's, not equal to it. For y = x^2 the limit works out to 2x.\n\nAn example \u2014 not your question, and the numbers are different:\nThe tangent to y = x^3 at x = 1.\nSecants from x = 1 to x = 1 + h have gradient ((1 + h)^3 - 1)/h,\nwhich multiplies out to 3 + 3h + h^2.\nAs h shrinks towards 0 that approaches 3.\nThe tangent's gradient is 3.",
      "opening": false,
      "version": 792
    },
    {
      "item_id": "ca_tan_p1",
      "concept_id": "limit_concept",
      "move": "present",
      "level": "formula_and_example",
      "targets": null,
      "mastery": 0.22941068023431924,
      "prior_failures": 0,
      "text": "The gradient of the tangent at a point is what the gradients of the secants approach as the second point slides in towards the first. A secant taken at any finite separation is still a secant: its gradient is close to the tangent's, not equal to it. For y = x^2 the limit works out to 2x.\n\nAn example \u2014 not your question, and the numbers are different:\nThe tangent to y = x^3 at x = 1.\nSecants from x = 1 to x = 1 + h have gradient ((1 + h)^3 - 1)/h,\nwhich multiplies out to 3 + 3h + h^2.\nAs h shrinks towards 0 that approaches 3.\nThe tangent's gradient is 3.",
      "opening": false,
      "version": 794
    }
  ],
